---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Tankless Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-tankless-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Tankless Water Heater Cost",
    "url": "/tankless-water-heater-cost/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Electric Tankless Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-electric-tankless-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Flush a Tankless Water Heater",
    "url": "/how-to-flush-a-tankless-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Water Heater Replacement Cost",
    "url": "/water-heater-replacement-cost/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Gas Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-gas-water-heater/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-20", modified: "2026-10-20" });
const title = "Best Propane Tankless Water Heaters: Whole-House and Portable";
const description =
  "Propane tankless water heaters for whole homes and camps, compared on BTU, GPM at your groundwater temperature, venting, tank size and LP conversion kits.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "rinnai-rsc199ip",
    "name": "Rinnai Sensei SE+ RSC199iP",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Whole-House, Condensing, Indoor, Propane",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The RSC199iP is a propane-specific Rinnai, so there is no conversion step to get wrong. Rinnai's product page lists a 15,000 to 199,000 BTU input range, an 11 GPM maximum hot water flow and a 0.95 UEF, with ENERGY STAR certification, Wi-Fi capability, recirculation capability and Ultra Low NOx approval. Retailer listings describe the SE+ series as including Rinnai's ThermaCirc360 recirculation. The wide 15,000 BTU minimum lets it modulate down for a single low-flow tap. It is a big unit at 68 pounds and 31.3 by 18.5 by 11.4 inches, uses three-quarter-inch gas and water connections, and needs a condensate drain. Rinnai publishes PVC, CPVC and polypropylene venting instructions for it. Remember that 11 GPM is a low-rise headline figure; in a cold-groundwater region expect roughly half that.",
    "pros": [
      "Dedicated propane model, no field conversion",
      "0.95 UEF and 199,000 BTU maximum per Rinnai",
      "Recirculation capable, ThermaCirc360 per retailer listings",
      "Large Rinnai dealer and service network"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "68 lb and over 31 inches tall",
      "Three-quarter-inch gas connection may mean a line upgrade",
      "Premium price class"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The propane tankless we would put in a two to three bathroom house with a properly sized tank, especially where recirculation matters."
  },
  {
    "id": "noritz-ez111dv",
    "name": "Noritz EZ111DV",
    "badge": "Best for Tank Replacement",
    "category": "Whole-House, Condensing, Indoor/Outdoor",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Noritz built the EZ series to replace a tank heater with as little replumbing as possible, which matters on a propane retrofit where the installer is already reworking the gas side. Noritz's page lists 0.4 to 11.1 GPM, 12,800 to 199,900 BTU and a 0.98 UEF, the highest efficiency figure in this list, with indoor and outdoor installation and a 25-year warranty. An NG to LP field conversion is included; Noritz says switching means swapping the provided orifice and selecting LP in its app. Hot and cold connections sit on top of the unit so a tank heater's flexible lines can be reconnected, and Noritz's EZTR tank-replacement bundles add a two-inch flex vent kit. Verify the propane configuration with your installer before ordering, since Noritz lists the natural gas model numbers.",
    "pros": [
      "0.98 UEF per Noritz, highest here",
      "LP conversion kit included",
      "Top-mounted water connections suit tank swaps",
      "25-year warranty per Noritz"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Ships for natural gas, so conversion must be done",
      "Fewer service techs than Rinnai in some areas",
      "Warranty terms depend on registration and install details"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The easiest whole-house propane upgrade from a tank heater, provided a qualified installer does the LP conversion."
  },
  {
    "id": "navien-npe-210a2",
    "name": "Navien NPE-210A2",
    "badge": "Best Built-In Recirculation",
    "category": "Whole-House, Condensing, Indoor",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The A2 version of Navien's NPE-2 adds a built-in buffer tank and recirculation pump, which is the cheapest way to cut the wait for hot water at a far bathroom and to smooth out the cold water sandwich. Navien's QuickFacts sheet lists 12,000 to 180,000 BTU on either natural gas or propane, a 0.95 UEF, and a rise table that gives 10.1 GPM at a 35 degree rise, 7.1 GPM at 50 degrees and 4.7 GPM at 75 degrees. Navien says all NPE-2 models are field convertible from natural gas to LP, and its propane conversion guide says the kit is supplied with the heater. Venting runs on two-inch pipe up to 75 feet. Navien's residential warranty listed on the same sheet is 15 years on the heat exchanger, 5 years on parts and 1 year on labor for standard or controlled recirculation.",
    "pros": [
      "Built-in buffer tank and recirculation pump",
      "Honest rise table published by Navien",
      "Two-inch venting up to 75 ft",
      "LP kit supplied, field convertible"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "180,000 BTU maximum, below the 199,000 BTU class",
      "Uncontrolled recirculation shortens the warranty",
      "Conversion required for propane"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it when the long wait for hot water bothers you more than peak flow, and the house is a two-bathroom size."
  },
  {
    "id": "rheem-rtgh-90dvlp-3",
    "name": "Rheem RTGH-90DVLP-3",
    "badge": "Best Value Condensing",
    "category": "Whole-House, Condensing, Indoor, Propane",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Rheem's RTGH-90DVLP-3 is the mid-size propane version of its super high efficiency condensing line. Rheem's product page lists up to a 0.93 UEF, Hot-Start Programming, an available outdoor conversion kit, and a 15-year heat exchanger, 5-year parts and 1-year labor warranty, and Ferguson lists a 0.4 to 9 GPM flow range. Rheem's RTGH-3 spec sheet rates the 90 size at 180,000 BTU maximum and 9.0 GPM at a 35 degree rise; that sheet is written for the natural gas sibling, so confirm the LP input with your installer. Venting is flexible, with Rheem quoting two-inch PVC up to 60 feet and three-inch up to 150 feet, and it measures about 23 by 18 by 9 inches. Availability varies; SupplyHouse showed it on backorder when we checked.",
    "pros": [
      "Dedicated propane SKU",
      "Up to 0.93 UEF per Rheem",
      "Two-inch PVC to 60 ft, three-inch to 150 ft",
      "Compact for a condensing unit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "9 GPM headline drops sharply at high rise",
      "No built-in recirculation pump",
      "Stock has been patchy at some retailers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible condensing LP heater for a one to two bathroom home where budget matters more than peak flow."
  },
  {
    "id": "rinnai-re180ep",
    "name": "Rinnai RE180eP",
    "badge": "Best Outdoor Mount",
    "category": "Whole-House, Non-Condensing, Outdoor, Propane",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Mounting outdoors removes the vent pipe and condensate drain entirely, which is often the cheapest installed whole-house propane option in a mild climate. Rinnai's page lists the RE180eP as an outdoor propane model with a 10,400 to 180,000 BTU input range, a 0.82 UEF, recirculation and Wi-Fi capability and a weight of 42.1 pounds. Rinnai does not show a maximum flow on that page; retailers list it at 8 to 8.5 GPM, so treat it as a two-bathroom unit in warm-groundwater areas. It is non-condensing, so it burns more propane per gallon heated than the condensing picks here. In freezing climates an outdoor unit depends on electric freeze protection, so a long power outage in a hard freeze is a real risk unless the unit is drained.",
    "pros": [
      "No vent pipe or condensate line needed",
      "Recirculation and Wi-Fi capable per Rinnai",
      "42.1 lb, easy to mount",
      "Rinnai service network"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "0.82 UEF burns more propane than condensing units",
      "Maximum flow comes from retailers, not Rinnai's page",
      "Freeze risk during power outages in cold climates"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right call in warm regions where an outside wall is near the tank and venting an indoor unit would be expensive."
  },
  {
    "id": "eccotemp-l5",
    "name": "Eccotemp L5 Portable",
    "badge": "Best Portable",
    "category": "Portable, Outdoor Only, 20 lb Cylinder",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The L5 is the portable propane water heater most people picture: a wall- or post-hung box that runs off a 20-pound grill cylinder and a garden hose. Eccotemp rates it at 1.5 GPM and 37,500 BTU, with outgoing temperature adjustable from 80 to 120 degrees, and says it needs at least half a gallon per minute of flow to ignite and run. Ignition runs on two D-cell batteries, and Eccotemp includes a CSA regulator. Eccotemp describes it as an outdoor-only unit. At 1.5 GPM it is one outdoor shower or a camp sink, not a house water heater. Some L5 variant listings, such as the L5-P and L5-PS, are marked discontinued at retailers, so check that the version you buy has current parts support.",
    "pros": [
      "1.5 GPM and 37,500 BTU per Eccotemp",
      "Runs on a standard 20 lb cylinder",
      "Battery ignition, no outlet needed",
      "Regulator included"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Outdoor only, never inside a cabin or RV",
      "Some variants discontinued at retailers",
      "Needs at least 0.5 GPM of flow to stay lit"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The portable to buy for camp showers and outdoor cleanup, as long as it stays outside."
  },
  {
    "id": "camplux-ay132",
    "name": "Camplux AY132 Portable",
    "badge": "Budget Portable",
    "category": "Portable, Outdoor Only, 20 lb Cylinder",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Camplux's AY132 is the smaller, cheaper alternative to the Eccotemp. Camplux's product pages list it as a 5-liter, 1.32 GPM unit with a 34,000 BTU burner, and listings say it starts at a low water pressure of about 3.0 psi, which helps on a gravity or RV pump supply. The base model has no built-in pump and needs a pressurized water supply; Camplux sells pump kit versions, such as the AY132BP43 listed at Wayfair, if you are drawing from a bucket or tank. Weight listings disagree, at 10 or 14 pounds. Like every portable here it is for outdoor use only. Treat it as a camp sink and quick-rinse heater rather than a full shower solution, because 1.32 GPM at a big temperature rise is a thin stream.",
    "pros": [
      "34,000 BTU, 1.32 GPM per Camplux",
      "Starts at about 3 psi water pressure",
      "Pump kit versions available",
      "Light and compact"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Lowest flow in this list",
      "Spec listings disagree on weight",
      "Outdoor only"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Fine for a camp kitchen or dog wash on a budget; buy the Eccotemp if showers are the main job."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Propane Tankless Water Heaters",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-propane-tankless-water-heater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size propane tankless water heater do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Work from your winter groundwater temperature and the fixtures that run at once. At a 75-degree rise, a 180,000 to 199,000 BTU condensing unit typically delivers about 4.5 to 5 GPM, enough for two showers. In warm-water regions the same unit covers three. Check the maker's rise table, not the headline GPM, and consider two cascaded units for big homes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How big a propane tank do I need for a tankless water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on climate and what else runs on the tank. Vaporization charts show tank output dropping sharply in cold weather, and one guide lists a 250-gallon tank at about 169,200 BTU per hour at zero degrees. Many suppliers put whole-house tankless systems on a 250 or 500 gallon tank. Ask your supplier to size it with the heater's maximum input."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I convert a natural gas tankless to propane?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often, yes. Navien says all NPE-2 models are field convertible and supplies the kit, and Noritz includes an LP conversion with the EZ111DV. Rinnai and Rheem sell many dedicated propane models instead. Either way, a qualified gas technician should do the conversion and check combustion afterward. Never run an unconverted natural gas unit on propane."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a propane tankless cheaper to run than a propane tank heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, because a tankless does not keep 40 or 50 gallons hot around the clock, and condensing units reach 0.93 to 0.98 UEF per their makers. The savings are larger on propane than on natural gas because the fuel costs more. Navien estimates about 196 gallons a year for its NPE-210A2 under the DOE test procedure; your use will differ."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a portable propane water heater be used indoors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Portable units like the Eccotemp L5 and Camplux AY132 are labelled for outdoor use only because they have no vent. Using one in a bathroom, cabin, camper or garage risks carbon monoxide poisoning. For indoor hot water you need a vented, permitted whole-house heater, or an electric point-of-use unit for a single sink."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do propane tankless water heaters need special venting?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Condensing units vent through PVC, CPVC or polypropylene, often with a concentric kit through one wall or roof hole, and need a condensate drain. Indoor non-condensing units usually need the maker's approved stainless or special vent system. Outdoor models such as the Rinnai RE180eP need no vent pipe. Clearances to windows, doors and the propane tank are set by the manual and code."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do propane tankless water heaters last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The makers here back heat exchangers for 15 years or more, and Noritz lists a 25-year warranty on the EZ111DV. Real life depends mostly on water hardness and maintenance. Descale annually in hard water, clean the inlet screen and keep the condensate drain clear. Skipping descaling is the most common way an expensive unit fails early."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "propane tankless water heater",
      "lp tankless water heater",
      "propane on demand water heater",
      "portable propane water heater",
      "outdoor propane tankless water heater",
      "propane tank size for tankless"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Propane Water Heating
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Propane Tankless <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Water Heaters</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Five whole-house LP units and two portable camp heaters, kept strictly apart, with the sizing, tank, regulator and venting math that decides whether any of them works at your house.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#rinnai-rsc199ip"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#whole-house-vs-portable" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Whole-House vs Portable</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing by BTU and GPM</a>
          <a href="#tank-size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Propane Tank Size</a>
          <a href="#gas-line" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Regulators and Gas Lines</a>
          <a href="#venting" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Venting</a>
          <a href="#conversion" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. LP Conversion Kits</a>
          <a href="#running-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Running Cost</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Safety and Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A propane tankless water heater is two very different products sold under one search term. One is a 199,000 BTU wall-hung appliance that heats a whole house and needs a properly sized tank, regulator and vent. The other is a 35,000 BTU box you hang on a post for a camp shower. Buy the wrong category and nothing else on this page matters.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose These Propane Units
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started from the propane-specific or propane-convertible models that the major tankless makers currently list, then checked each against the maker's own product page or spec sheet. A surprising number of LP units that still appear in older roundups are gone. Takagi's T-H3-DV-P, for example, is marked discontinued at SupplyHouse, and Rinnai's V65eP is marked discontinued at Build.com even though Rinnai still has a page for it. We left both off rather than send you hunting for old stock.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also deliberately avoided repeating our main <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">best tankless water heater</a> list. That guide is written around natural gas and covers models like the Rinnai RX199iN and Navien NPE-240A2. Several of those run on propane too, and if one of them suits you it is still a good choice, but this page focuses on the propane-specific SKUs and the smaller sizes that fit a typical LP household.
          </p>

          <p>
            The criteria were simple. A whole-house pick had to publish its BTU range, maximum flow, efficiency and propane compatibility, and it had to be something a local installer can actually buy and service. A portable pick had to publish its BTU input and flow and be clearly labelled for outdoor use. We did not test any of these units; where we quote a figure we say whose figure it is, and where sources disagree we tell you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-house-vs-portable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Whole-House vs Portable: Two Different Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole-house propane tankless heater burns up to roughly 180,000 to 200,000 BTU per hour, mounts on a wall, connects to your plumbing and gas system, and vents combustion gases outside through a dedicated pipe or by being mounted outdoors. It is installed under a plumbing and gas permit in most places, and it is meant to run for 15 to 20 years. The five whole-house picks below are in this class.
          </p>

          <p>
            A portable propane water heater is a small burner, usually 30,000 to 40,000 BTU, that screws onto a 20-pound grill cylinder and a garden hose. Eccotemp rates its L5 at 1.5 gallons per minute and Camplux rates its AY132 at 1.32 GPM. That is enough for one modest outdoor shower, washing dishes at camp or rinsing a dog, but it is less than a single standard shower head flows in a house. These units have no vent, which is exactly why both makers say outdoors only.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never take a portable propane heater indoors.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Portable units burn propane in open air with no flue. Used in a bathroom, cabin, RV, tent or garage they can produce deadly carbon monoxide. The CPSC warns that carbon monoxide is invisible and odorless. If you need hot water inside, you need a vented whole-house unit installed by a professional.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is a middle case worth naming: an off-grid cabin or tiny house. People are tempted to hang a portable unit inside to save money. Do not. Either mount a portable unit outside with the plumbing run through the wall, or buy a small vented whole-house unit such as an outdoor-mount model. If the cabin is all-electric, a <a href="/point-of-use-water-heater/">point-of-use water heater</a> may be simpler than propane at all.
          </p>
        </div>

