        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: BTU, GPM and Your Groundwater Temperature
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every tankless heater is limited by how many degrees it can raise a given flow of water. The physics is a single line: gallons per minute equals BTU output divided by 500 times the temperature rise. The 500 comes from a gallon of water weighing about 8.33 pounds, times 60 minutes. Output is input times efficiency, so a 199,000 BTU condensing unit at about 95 percent delivers roughly 189,000 BTU of heat into the water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature rise is the number that changes everything. If you want 120 degree water and your incoming water is 45 degrees, as it often is in northern winters, you need a 75 degree rise. In the Gulf states incoming water can sit near 65 to 70 degrees, which needs only a 50 to 55 degree rise. The same heater can deliver almost half again as much hot water in Florida as it does in Minnesota.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Temperature rise</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Navien NPE-210A2</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Navien NPE-240A2</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">35°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10.1 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11.2 GPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7.1 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7.8 GPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">60°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.9 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.5 GPM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">75°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.7 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.2 GPM</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That table comes straight from Navien's QuickFacts sheet, and it is the honest way to read any maximum GPM figure. The headline numbers on the box, 10 or 11 GPM, are quoted at a 35 degree rise, which almost nobody has in winter. At a 75 degree rise the NPE-210A2 delivers 4.7 GPM. That is two showers running at about 2 GPM each, which is about right for many houses.
          </p>

          <p>
            To size yours, add up the fixtures that genuinely run together on a cold morning, use your winter inlet temperature, and check the maker's rise table rather than the headline GPM. Our guide on <a href="/what-size-tankless-water-heater-do-i-need/">what size tankless water heater you need</a> walks through the fixture count in more detail. If the numbers say you need more than one unit can deliver, most makers allow two units to be cascaded.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tank-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Propane Tank Size and Why Cylinders Are Not Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            Propane leaves a tank as vapor, and the tank can only boil off liquid so fast. That vaporization rate falls as the weather gets colder and as the tank empties, and it is the most common reason a propane tankless heater that works fine in July sputters in January. A whole-house unit asking for 199,000 BTU per hour needs a tank that can actually supply it on the coldest morning, alongside any furnace, range or dryer on the same tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Published charts vary, but they all point the same way. One tank-sizing guide from Elliott Electric lists a 120-gallon tank at about 246,000 BTU per hour at 40 degrees, while a 250-gallon tank drops from about 507,600 BTU per hour at 40 degrees to about 169,200 at zero. That zero-degree figure is below a single 199,000 BTU heater running flat out, before the furnace is counted.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Propane Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~91,500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">BTU in a gallon of propane</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~2.2 gal/hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Burn at 199,000 BTU full fire</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~4.7 gal</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Propane in a full 20 lb cylinder</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">11 in. WC</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical house-side LP pressure</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            In practice that is why propane suppliers usually put a whole-house tankless on a 250 or 500 gallon tank, and why a pair of 100-pound cylinders is a poor fit in cold climates. Tankless heaters only burn at full fire while hot water is running, so total gallons used per month are modest, but the instantaneous draw is large. Tell your supplier the heater's maximum BTU input and every other appliance on the tank, and ask them to size the tank and regulator for your coldest month.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable units are the opposite case. At 37,500 BTU the Eccotemp L5 uses about four tenths of a gallon per hour at full fire, so a full 20-pound cylinder, which holds about 4.7 gallons, covers roughly ten hours of burner time. Bring a spare cylinder for a week of camp showers, and expect small cylinders to lose pressure in near-freezing weather.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-line" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Regulators, Gas Pressure and Line Sizing
          </h2>

          <p>
            A residential propane system normally uses two-stage regulation: a first-stage regulator at the tank drops tank pressure to roughly 10 psi for the run to the house, and a second-stage regulator near the house drops it to about 11 inches of water column for the appliances. Every tankless manual prints a minimum and maximum inlet pressure, and a heater asked to run on undersized supply pressure will throw ignition or flame codes at high demand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The regulator has a capacity rating, too. If your existing second-stage regulator was sized for a furnace and a 40,000 BTU tank water heater, adding a 199,000 BTU tankless can exceed it. The same goes for the pipe. Propane carries roughly two and a half times the energy of natural gas per cubic foot, so LP lines can be smaller than natural gas lines for the same BTU, but a long half-inch run feeding a whole-house tankless and a furnace can still starve the heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some makers advertise easier gas sizing. Navien's QuickFacts sheet promotes half-inch gas pipe compatibility over runs of up to about 24 feet on the NPE-2 series, while the RSC199iP and Noritz EZ111DV use three-quarter-inch gas connections. None of this replaces a load calculation. The installer should total every appliance on the system, measure the run, size pipe from the code tables in your jurisdiction and, ideally, check pressure at the heater with everything firing.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not DIY work. A gas line, regulator and appliance connection need a licensed gas fitter or plumber, a leak test and in most places a permit and inspection. If you are pricing the job, our <a href="/tankless-water-heater-cost/">tankless water heater cost</a> guide covers what installers typically charge for gas line upgrades, and the <a href="/water-heater-replacement-cost/">water heater replacement cost</a> guide compares that against simply swapping a tank.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Venting: Concentric PVC, Stainless or Outdoors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Condensing propane units pull so much heat out of the exhaust that it leaves cool enough for plastic pipe. Rinnai's product pages list PVC, CPVC and polypropylene venting instructions, and Rheem's RTGH literature quotes two-inch PVC runs of up to 60 feet and three-inch runs of up to 150 feet. Navien quotes two-inch venting up to 75 feet. Many installs use a concentric kit, a pipe within a pipe that brings combustion air in around the exhaust through a single wall or roof penetration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Condensing units also make condensate, an acidic water that has to drain somewhere. Plan a drain route and, where local code or the manual requires it, a neutralizer cartridge. Our explainer on <a href="/condensing-vs-non-condensing-tankless-water-heater/">condensing vs non-condensing tankless</a> heaters covers the trade-offs in more depth, but for propane the answer is usually condensing because it burns less of an expensive fuel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoor non-condensing units, such as Rinnai's RE199iP, vent hotter exhaust and usually need the maker's approved stainless or special venting system rather than PVC, which often wipes out their lower sticker price. The cleaner way to use a non-condensing unit is outdoors. An outdoor model like the RE180eP mounts on an exterior wall with no vent pipe and no condensate line at all, which is often the cheapest installed option in mild climates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not reuse an old tank heater's metal flue for any tankless unit unless the manual specifically allows it, and never vent into a chimney shared with another appliance. Clearances to windows, doors, soffits and the propane tank itself are spelled out in the manual and in fuel gas code, and they are a frequent inspection failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="conversion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. LP Conversion Kits: Convert or Buy the LP Model?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Makers handle propane in two ways. Rinnai and Rheem sell many models as separate propane SKUs, identifiable by a P in the model number, such as the RSC199iP or RTGH-90DVLP-3. Navien and Noritz ship most models ready for natural gas with a propane conversion kit in the box. Navien's QuickFacts sheet says all NPE-2 models are field convertible from natural gas to LP, and Noritz lists an NG to LP field gas conversion as included with the EZ111DV.
          </p>

          <p>
            A conversion kit typically swaps the gas orifice or nozzle and changes a setting, a DIP switch or an app parameter, so the controls know the fuel. Navien's guide specifically warns installers not to confuse its propane kit with its high-altitude kit, which is for natural gas only. The work also usually requires a combustion check with an analyzer afterward, and a conversion label on the unit.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WARRANTY AND SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A wrong or skipped conversion is dangerous.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Running a natural gas configured heater on propane over-fires it, which can produce soot, carbon monoxide and a damaged heat exchanger. Navien's spec sheet says conversion requires a qualified gas servicer. Treat that as the rule for every brand.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For a buyer, the practical difference is small. A dedicated LP model removes one step and one opportunity for error. A convertible model is handy if your street may get natural gas later or if your local supplier only stocks the NG version. Either way, tell the installer you are on propane before the unit is ordered, so the right kit or SKU arrives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Running Cost: Propane vs Natural Gas vs Electric
          </h2>

          <p>
            Propane is usually the expensive fuel. Navien's own QuickFacts sheet estimates the NPE-210A2 at 179 therms a year on natural gas, about $195 at $1.09 a therm, versus 196 gallons a year on propane, about $472 at $2.41 a gallon. Your local prices will differ, but the ratio is typical: the same hot water costs roughly twice as much on LP as on natural gas.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the single best argument for a condensing unit on propane. A 0.95 UEF condensing heater turns more of each expensive gallon into hot water than a 0.82 UEF non-condensing model. On a fuel that costs more than double natural gas per unit of heat, the efficiency gap pays back faster than it does in our natural gas comparisons, especially in a household with heavy hot water use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric is the other option. A heat pump water heater can beat propane on running cost in many regions, and an electric tankless can work in warm climates if the panel can handle it. Our <a href="/best-electric-tankless-water-heater/">electric tankless water heater</a> guide covers the panel math. If you already have propane for heating and a 200-amp panel without spare capacity, propane tankless is often the realistic choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            One cost that propane buyers forget is the tank. If you lease a tank from your supplier, ask whether adding a large tankless changes the tank size, regulator or delivery schedule. Owning versus leasing the tank also affects which supplier can fill it, which affects your per-gallon price for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Safety, Code and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before any work on a gas water heater, shut off the gas at the appliance valve, switch off and unplug the electrical supply, and close the water isolation valves. Tankless units can deliver scalding water immediately, so keep the set point at 120 degrees unless you have a specific reason and a mixing valve. Let the heat exchanger cool before opening anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most tankless installation manuals still call for a pressure relief valve on the hot outlet, and isolation valve kits commonly include one. Like the T and P valve on a tank heater, it must never be plugged, capped or removed, and its discharge line must run to a safe location. Our <a href="/water-heater-pressure-relief-valve/">water heater pressure relief valve</a> guide covers testing and replacement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Install a carbon monoxide alarm on every level of a home with gas appliances, and a propane leak detector if the heater is in a basement, since propane is heavier than air and pools low. If you smell gas, leave the house and call your propane supplier or 911 from outside. Do not try to find the leak yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is mostly descaling. In hard water, flush the heat exchanger with a pump and vinegar or the maker's descaler once a year using the isolation valves; our <a href="/how-to-flush-a-tankless-water-heater/">how to flush a tankless water heater</a> guide has the steps. Clean the inlet water screen, check the condensate drain and neutralizer, and have the gas pressure and combustion checked by a technician every few years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Portable for Indoor Use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Portable propane heaters have no flue. Hanging one in a cabin bathroom, RV or garage risks carbon monoxide poisoning. Eccotemp and Camplux label their units for outdoor use, and that is not a formality. If you need hot water indoors, budget for a vented whole-house unit installed by a licensed pro.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing From the 35-Degree GPM Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Headline flow ratings are quoted at a small temperature rise. In a cold-water region a heater sold as 10 GPM may deliver under 5 GPM in winter. Use your winter inlet temperature and the maker's rise table, such as Navien's, before deciding how many showers one unit can run.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Tank Vaporization</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 199,000 BTU heater can outrun a small tank or a pair of 100-pound cylinders on a cold morning, especially with a furnace on the same supply. Give your propane supplier the heater's maximum input and every other appliance, and let them size the tank and regulators for your coldest month.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reusing the Old Regulator and Gas Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A regulator and half-inch line sized for a tank heater and furnace may not carry a whole-house tankless. Under-supplied heaters throw ignition codes when demand peaks. Have the installer do a full load calculation, size pipe from code tables and check pressure at the heater under load.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">DIY Propane Conversion</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Conversion kits look simple, an orifice and a setting, but a mistake can over-fire the burner, produce soot and carbon monoxide and ruin the heat exchanger. Navien says conversion requires a qualified gas servicer. Pay for the conversion and a combustion analysis; it is a small part of the installed cost.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

