        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size propane tankless water heater do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Work from your winter groundwater temperature and the fixtures that run at once. At a 75-degree rise, a 180,000 to 199,000 BTU condensing unit typically delivers about 4.5 to 5 GPM, enough for two showers. In warm-water regions the same unit covers three. Check the maker's rise table, not the headline GPM, and consider two cascaded units for big homes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a propane tank do I need for a tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on climate and what else runs on the tank. Vaporization charts show tank output dropping sharply in cold weather, and one guide lists a 250-gallon tank at about 169,200 BTU per hour at zero degrees. Many suppliers put whole-house tankless systems on a 250 or 500 gallon tank. Ask your supplier to size it with the heater's maximum input.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I convert a natural gas tankless to propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Navien says all NPE-2 models are field convertible and supplies the kit, and Noritz includes an LP conversion with the EZ111DV. Rinnai and Rheem sell many dedicated propane models instead. Either way, a qualified gas technician should do the conversion and check combustion afterward. Never run an unconverted natural gas unit on propane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a propane tankless cheaper to run than a propane tank heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, because a tankless does not keep 40 or 50 gallons hot around the clock, and condensing units reach 0.93 to 0.98 UEF per their makers. The savings are larger on propane than on natural gas because the fuel costs more. Navien estimates about 196 gallons a year for its NPE-210A2 under the DOE test procedure; your use will differ.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable propane water heater be used indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Portable units like the Eccotemp L5 and Camplux AY132 are labelled for outdoor use only because they have no vent. Using one in a bathroom, cabin, camper or garage risks carbon monoxide poisoning. For indoor hot water you need a vented, permitted whole-house heater, or an electric point-of-use unit for a single sink.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do propane tankless water heaters need special venting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Condensing units vent through PVC, CPVC or polypropylene, often with a concentric kit through one wall or roof hole, and need a condensate drain. Indoor non-condensing units usually need the maker's approved stainless or special vent system. Outdoor models such as the Rinnai RE180eP need no vent pipe. Clearances to windows, doors and the propane tank are set by the manual and code.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do propane tankless water heaters last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The makers here back heat exchangers for 15 years or more, and Noritz lists a 25-year warranty on the EZ111DV. Real life depends mostly on water hardness and maintenance. Descale annually in hard water, clean the inlet screen and keep the condensate drain clear. Skipping descaling is the most common way an expensive unit fails early.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
